DSP.mod(9,{"m9-time-series":{"id":"m9-time-series","slug":"time-series-forecasting","m":9,"title":"Time series forecasting","min":45,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":["m7-leakage"],"tags":["time-series","forecasting"],"qa":9,"ai":{"scope":"Time series forecasting: components, stationarity and differencing, ACF/PACF, baselines and models (ARIMA, Prophet, ML with lags), time-aware validation and metrics, with a worked SARIMA comparison.","keyTerms":["trend","seasonality","stationarity","ADF test","ACF and PACF","ARIMA","SARIMA","Prophet","rolling-origin validation","seasonal naive baseline","lag features"],"interviewQs":["Why can't you shuffle time series data?","What is stationarity and how do you test and achieve it?","How do you validate a forecasting model?","How would you forecast demand for a new product with no history?"],"mistakes":["Random train-test split on time series","Lag or rolling features that include the current value (leakage)","Not comparing against a seasonal naive baseline"],"practice":"Build naive, seasonal naive and SARIMA forecasts on a synthetic weekly-seasonal series and compare MAE.","related":["m7-leakage","m8-other-metrics","m8-feature-engineering"],"points":"Concepts\n\n- Components: trend, seasonality, cyclic, residual/noise. Additive vs multiplicative.\n- Stationarity: constant mean, variance, autocovariance. Test with ADF (H0: non-stationary) and KPSS (H0: stationary). Achieve via differencing, log transform, detrending.\n- ACF/PACF: identify AR (PACF cuts off) and MA (ACF cuts off) orders.\n- Models: naive/seasonal naive baselines, moving average, exponential smoothing (Holt-Winters), ARIMA(p,d,q) / SARIMA, Prophet (trend + seasonality + holidays), ML with lag features (LightGBM), deep models (LSTM, N-BEATS, TFT) for big data.\n- Validation: never random split. Use rolling-origin / expanding-window (TimeSeriesSplit). Forecast horizon should match production use.\n- Metrics: MAE, RMSE, MAPE/sMAPE, MASE, WAPE; compare vs naive baseline.\n- Feature engineering for ML-based forecasting: lags, rolling mean/std, day of week, holidays/festivals (Diwali, IPL, sale events), price, promotions, weather, hierarchy (store, SKU).\n- Common Indian use cases: demand forecasting for quick commerce/retail, ETA prediction, ride demand, electricity load, UPI transaction volumes, inventory planning.\n\nQ: Why can't we shuffle time-series data?\nA: Breaks temporal order and leaks future into past.\n\nQ: What if the series has multiple seasonalities?\nA: TBATS, Prophet, Fourier terms, ML with calendar features.\n\nQ: How do you forecast new products with no history (cold start)?\nA: Use category-level/similar-item priors, hierarchical models."},"intuitive":"<p>Forecasting is predicting tomorrow from a diary of yesterdays. The diary has a slow drift up or down (trend), a repeating rhythm like busier weekends or festival spikes (seasonality), and random wobble (noise). The simplest forecast, &quot;next week will look like this week&quot;, is surprisingly hard to beat, so every model must prove itself against it. Most important rule: never peek into the future when training. That means you test on the most recent period, not on random days scattered through the diary.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Demand, ETA, electricity load and UPI volume forecasting are common Indian use cases, and time-awareness is a favourite interview check.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Decompose, make the series stationary if needed, beat the seasonal naive baseline, validate forward in time.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Time series data is ordered in time, so order matters and random splits are wrong. I look at the trend, seasonality, and noise. Stationarity, a stable mean and variance, matters for classical models; I difference to remove trend. ARIMA models the series by its own past values and past errors; SARIMA adds seasonality. Modern approaches build lag features, rolling means and calendar features, and use gradient boosting; Prophet handles holidays well. Baselines come first: the last value, or the same day last week. Validation is forward-chaining. The catch: leakage through future information, and judging models by one split rather than several time windows.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Two baselines every forecast must beat","html":"<p>Daily orders show a weekly pattern. Last week, Monday to Sunday: 100, 90, 95, 105, 130, 160, 150. This week&#39;s actuals: 104, 92, 99, 108, 135, 165, 148.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Naive forecast:</strong> tomorrow equals today. Monday&#39;s forecast is last Sunday&#39;s 150; Tuesday&#39;s is Monday&#39;s 104, and so on.</li>\n<li><strong>Seasonal naive forecast:</strong> each day equals the same day last week: Monday 100, Tuesday 90, and so on.</li>\n<li><strong>Errors (mean absolute error over the week):</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Actual</th><th>Naive</th><th>Seasonal naive</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mon</td><td>104</td><td>150</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>Tue</td><td>92</td><td>104</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><td>Wed</td><td>99</td><td>92</td><td>95</td></tr><tr><td>Thu</td><td>108</td><td>99</td><td>105</td></tr><tr><td>Fri</td><td>135</td><td>108</td><td>130</td></tr><tr><td>Sat</td><td>165</td><td>135</td><td>160</td></tr><tr><td>Sun</td><td>148</td><td>165</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MAE</strong></td><td></td><td><strong>21.1</strong></td><td><strong>3.6</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li><strong>Read it.</strong> The naive forecast is poor because it ignores the weekly rhythm: it always forecasts the weekend from Friday. The seasonal naive forecast is already within 4 orders a day.</li>\n<li><strong>The bar for any model:</strong> an MAE well below 3.6 on several future weeks, not just one.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Before building a model, compute the dumb forecasts. If the series is seasonal, &quot;same day last week&quot; is a surprisingly strong opponent.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-components-and-stationarity","text":"Components and stationarity","level":3},{"id":"h-acf-and-pacf-for-choosing-orders","text":"ACF and PACF for choosing orders","level":3},{"id":"h-the-model-families","text":"The model families","level":3},{"id":"h-a-worked-comparison","text":"A worked comparison","level":3},{"id":"h-feature-engineering-for-ml-based-forecasting","text":"Feature engineering for ML-based forecasting","level":3},{"id":"h-validation-and-metrics","text":"Validation and metrics","level":3},{"id":"h-indian-use-cases","text":"Indian use cases","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Concepts</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Components: trend, seasonality, cyclic, residual/noise. Additive vs multiplicative.</li>\n<li><strong>Stationarity:</strong> constant mean, variance, autocovariance. Test with <strong>ADF</strong> (H0: non-stationary) and KPSS (H0: stationary). Achieve via differencing, log transform, detrending.</li>\n<li><strong>ACF/PACF:</strong> identify AR (PACF cuts off) and MA (ACF cuts off) orders.</li>\n<li><strong>Models:</strong> naive/seasonal naive baselines, moving average, exponential smoothing (Holt-Winters), <strong>ARIMA(p,d,q) / SARIMA</strong>, <strong>Prophet</strong> (trend + seasonality + holidays), ML with lag features (LightGBM), deep models (LSTM, N-BEATS, TFT) for big data.</li>\n<li><strong>Validation:</strong> never random split. Use <strong>rolling-origin / expanding-window (<code>TimeSeriesSplit</code>)</strong>. Forecast horizon should match production use.</li>\n<li><strong>Metrics:</strong> MAE, RMSE, MAPE/sMAPE, MASE, WAPE; compare vs naive baseline.</li>\n<li><strong>Feature engineering for ML-based forecasting:</strong> lags, rolling mean/std, day of week, holidays/festivals (Diwali, IPL, sale events), price, promotions, weather, hierarchy (store, SKU).</li>\n<li><strong>Common Indian use cases:</strong> demand forecasting for quick commerce/retail, ETA prediction, ride demand, electricity load, UPI transaction volumes, inventory planning.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.tsa.stattools <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> adfuller\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.tsa.statespace.sarimax <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SARIMAX\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.model_selection <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> TimeSeriesSplit\n\nadf_p = adfuller(series.dropna())[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]         <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># p &lt; 0.05 -&gt; stationary</span>\nmodel = SARIMAX(series, order=(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>), seasonal_order=(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>)).fit(disp=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)\nforecast = model.forecast(<span class=\"hljs-number\">14</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Lag features for ML</span>\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;lag_1&#x27;</span>] = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;y&#x27;</span>].shift(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;lag_7&#x27;</span>] = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;y&#x27;</span>].shift(<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>)\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;roll_7_mean&#x27;</span>] = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;y&#x27;</span>].shift(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>).rolling(<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>).mean()   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># shift(1) prevents leakage</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"9.1#1","src":"c","q":"Why can&#39;t we shuffle time-series data?","a":"<p>Breaks temporal order and leaks future into past.</p>\n","s":"Shuffling breaks temporal order and lets the future leak into the past."},{"k":"qa","id":"9.1#2","src":"c","q":"What if the series has multiple seasonalities?","a":"<p>TBATS, Prophet, Fourier terms, ML with calendar features.</p>\n","s":"TBATS, Prophet, Fourier terms, or ML models with calendar features."},{"k":"qa","id":"9.1#3","src":"c","q":"How do you forecast new products with no history (cold start)?","a":"<p>Use category-level/similar-item priors, hierarchical models.</p>\n","s":"Use category-level or similar-item priors and hierarchical models, updating as data arrives."},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-components-and-stationarity\">Components and stationarity</h3>\n<p>A series is often <strong>trend + seasonality + cyclical + noise</strong>; <strong>additive</strong> if the seasonal swings have constant size, <strong>multiplicative</strong> if they grow with the level (then take logs).</p>\n<p><strong>Stationary</strong> means constant mean, variance and autocovariance over time. Many classical models (ARIMA) assume it. Tests: <strong>ADF</strong> (H₀: a unit root, <em>non-stationary</em>; a small p-value suggests stationarity) and <strong>KPSS</strong> (H₀: stationary). Achieve it by <strong>differencing</strong> (subtract the previous value; seasonal differencing subtracts the value one season ago), log transforms and detrending.</p>\n<p>On a synthetic daily series with trend and a weekly cycle, the ADF p-value for the raw series is 0.83 (non-stationary), but after first differencing it is 5e-6 (stationary).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-acf-and-pacf-for-choosing-orders\">ACF and PACF for choosing orders</h3>\n<p>The <strong>ACF</strong> shows correlation with each lag; the <strong>PACF</strong> shows the correlation at a lag after removing the effect of shorter lags. Rules of thumb: an <strong>AR(p)</strong> process has a PACF that <strong>cuts off after lag p</strong> (and a decaying ACF); an <strong>MA(q)</strong> process has an ACF that cuts off after lag q. For a simulated AR(1) with coefficient 0.7 the ACF decays (1.0, 0.67, 0.43, 0.27, ...) while the PACF is 0.67 at lag 1 and about 0 afterwards.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-model-families\">The model families</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Idea</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Naive / seasonal naive</strong></td><td>repeat the last value / the value one season ago</td><td>baselines you must beat</td></tr><tr><td>Moving average, <strong>exponential smoothing</strong> (Holt-Winters)</td><td>weighted averages; trend and seasonal components</td><td>simple, strong for regular series</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ARIMA(p, d, q)</strong> / <strong>SARIMA</strong></td><td>autoregression, differencing (d), moving average; seasonal terms</td><td>single series with stable patterns</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prophet</strong></td><td>additive trend + seasonality + holidays (handles missing data, changepoints)</td><td>business series with holidays</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ML with lag features</strong> (LightGBM)</td><td>regression on lags, rolling stats, calendar, price, promotions</td><td>many related series, external drivers</td></tr><tr><td>Deep models (LSTM, N-BEATS, TFT)</td><td>sequence networks</td><td>very large datasets</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-worked-comparison\">A worked comparison</h3>\n<p>Forecast the last 14 days of a daily series with trend and weekly seasonality (MAE in units of the series):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>MAE</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Naive (repeat the last value)</td><td>10.2</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Seasonal naive</strong> (repeat the last week)</td><td>3.7</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SARIMA(1,1,1)(1,1,1,7)</strong></td><td><strong>1.9</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.tsa.stattools <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> adfuller\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.tsa.statespace.sarimax <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SARIMAX\n\nadf_p = adfuller(series.dropna())[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]                                 <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># p &lt; 0.05 suggests stationarity</span>\nmodel = SARIMAX(train, order=(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>), seasonal_order=(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>)).fit(disp=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)\nforecast = model.forecast(<span class=\"hljs-number\">14</span>)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Note how much of the gain came from just respecting the weekly seasonality (10.2 to 3.7) before any clever modelling.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-feature-engineering-for-ml-based-forecasting\">Feature engineering for ML-based forecasting</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df = df.sort_values(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;date&quot;</span>)\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;lag_1&quot;</span>] = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;y&quot;</span>].shift(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;lag_7&quot;</span>] = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;y&quot;</span>].shift(<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>)\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;roll_7_mean&quot;</span>] = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;y&quot;</span>].shift(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>).rolling(<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>).mean()          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># shift(1) BEFORE rolling so today&#x27;s value is not in its own feature</span>\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dow&quot;</span>] = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;date&quot;</span>].dt.dayofweek</code></pre></div>\n<p>Add calendar effects (day of week, month, <strong>Indian festivals</strong> such as Diwali and Eid, IPL match days, sale events like Big Billion Days), price, promotions, weather, and hierarchy keys (store, SKU, city). Predict multiple horizons directly or recursively, and keep features that will be known at forecast time (future prices and promotions planned; weather forecasts rather than actual weather).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-validation-and-metrics\">Validation and metrics</h3>\n<p><strong>Never shuffle.</strong> Use <strong>rolling-origin (expanding-window) validation</strong>: train up to time t, forecast the next h steps, slide forward, repeat (<code>TimeSeriesSplit</code>). Match the forecast horizon to how the forecast is used. Metrics: MAE, RMSE, <strong>WAPE</strong>, sMAPE, <strong>MASE</strong> (error scaled by the naive error: below 1 beats the naive), bias; always compare with the <strong>seasonal naive</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.model_selection <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> TimeSeriesSplit\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> tr, va <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> TimeSeriesSplit(n_splits=<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, test_size=<span class=\"hljs-number\">14</span>).split(X):      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># the training indices are always earlier than the validation indices</span>\n    ...</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-indian-use-cases\">Indian use cases</h3>\n<p>Demand forecasting for quick commerce and retail, ETA prediction, ride demand, electricity load, UPI transaction volumes, and inventory planning.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m9-time-series~1","q":"How do you forecast demand for a newly launched product with no history (cold start)?","a":"<p>Use <strong>analogous items</strong> (products with similar attributes, price and launch patterns) and <strong>category-level forecasts</strong> scaled by expected share; train a global ML model with product attributes (category, brand, price, pack size) as features so new items get predictions from their attributes; use <strong>hierarchical forecasting</strong> to reconcile levels; apply <strong>Bayesian updating</strong> or a fast-adapting smoother as the first observations arrive; and plan with wider uncertainty ranges and conservative inventory while learning.</p>\n","s":"Borrow from similar products or the category, use hierarchical or attribute-based models, and update quickly as data arrives."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m9-time-series~2","q":"Your series has both weekly and yearly seasonality. Which approaches handle multiple seasonalities?","a":"<p>SARIMA handles one seasonal period. For several seasonalities use <strong>Fourier terms</strong> (sine/cosine pairs for each period) in a regression or ARIMA-with-regressors, <strong>Prophet</strong> (additive seasonal components), <strong>TBATS</strong>, or <strong>ML/deep models</strong> with calendar features (day of week, day of year, holiday flags) and lags.</p>\n","s":"Prophet, TBATS, Fourier terms in regression, or ML models with calendar features."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-time-series~ca0_1","q":"What baseline should any forecast beat?","a":"<p>Naive forecasts: yesterday&#39;s value, or the value from the same weekday last week (seasonal naive).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-time-series~ca0_2","q":"How do you make a trending series stationary?","a":"<p>Difference it (subtract the previous value), possibly after a log transform to stabilise variance; test with ADF.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-time-series~ca0_3","q":"Why use rolling-origin cross-validation?","a":"<p>Several train-past, test-future windows give a more reliable error estimate and respect time order.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m9-time-series~ta1_1","q":"<p>Your gradient-boosting forecast has an MAE of 4.5 on the same week, against 3.6 for seasonal naive. The scaled error (MASE) is your MAE divided by the naive benchmark&#39;s MAE. What is it, and what do you conclude?</p>\n","a":"<p>MASE = 4.5 / 3.6 ≈ 1.25. A value above 1 means the model is worse than the simple benchmark, so it is not ready, however sophisticated it is. Check the features (does it know the weekday and holidays?), the validation set-up and possible leakage, and compare over several weeks, not one.</p>\n"}]},"m9-clustering":{"id":"m9-clustering","slug":"clustering","m":9,"title":"Clustering","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":["m8-preprocessing"],"tags":["clustering","unsupervised"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Clustering algorithms (k-means, hierarchical, DBSCAN, GMM), choosing k with elbow and silhouette, validation and profiling, with a numeric example.","keyTerms":["k-means","k-means++","hierarchical clustering","DBSCAN","Gaussian mixture","elbow","silhouette","RFM segmentation","scaling"],"interviewQs":["How does k-means work and what are its assumptions?","How do you choose k?","When would you prefer DBSCAN over k-means?"],"mistakes":["Not scaling features","Choosing k only by the elbow","Treating clusters as ground truth instead of a useful segmentation"],"practice":"Cluster customers on RFM features, choose k with silhouette, and write a one-line persona for each cluster.","related":["m1-scratch-ml","m8-dim-reduction","m2-level-5b"],"points":"Algorithm - Idea - Pros - Cons\nK-Means - Assign to nearest centroid; update centroids; repeat - Fast, simple - Needs k, assumes spherical equal-size clusters, sensitive to scale/outliers/init (use k-means++)\nHierarchical (agglomerative) - Merge closest clusters; dendrogram - No k upfront, structure view - O(n^2) memory/time\nDBSCAN - Density-based: core points within eps and min_samples - Finds arbitrary shapes, detects noise - Struggles with varying density; eps tuning\nGMM - Soft clustering with Gaussians via EM - Soft assignments, elliptical clusters - Needs k, sensitive to init\n\n- Choosing k: elbow, silhouette, gap statistic, business usability.\n- Always scale features; consider PCA before clustering in high dimensions.\n- Validate: silhouette, stability across runs, profiling clusters on business variables.\n- Use cases: customer segmentation (RFM), store clustering, content grouping."},"intuitive":"<p>Clustering sorts things into groups without being told the groups in advance, like sorting a pile of mixed buttons by similarity. K-means drops k flags and keeps moving them until each flag sits at the centre of the buttons nearest to it. DBSCAN instead finds crowded neighbourhoods and treats lonely buttons as noise, so it can find odd shapes. The hard parts are deciding how many groups there are and checking that they are useful, not just mathematically neat.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Customer segmentation (RFM), store clustering and content grouping are everyday tasks.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Scale first, pick k by several signals plus business sense, and always profile the clusters.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Clustering groups similar points without labels. K-means picks k centres, assigns each point to the nearest, moves each centre to its group&#39;s mean, and repeats. It is fast but assumes round, similar-sized clusters, needs k up front, and is sensitive to scale and starting points. I choose k with the elbow of inertia or the silhouette score. Hierarchical clustering builds a tree you can cut at any level. DBSCAN groups dense regions, finds odd shapes and labels noise, but struggles with varying densities. The catch: there is no ground truth, so the cluster usefulness is judged by interpretability and business action, not only a score.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Inertia and silhouette by hand","html":"<p>Five customers on one feature (orders per month): 1, 2, 3, 8, 9.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Inertia</strong> (the sum of squared distances to each point&#39;s cluster centre) for different k:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>k</th><th>Clusters</th><th>Inertia</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>{1, 2, 3, 8, 9}</td><td>53.2</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>{1, 2, 3}, {8, 9}</td><td>2.5</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>{1, 2, 3}, {8}, {9}</td><td>2.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>The elbow.</strong> Going from 1 to 2 clusters removes almost all the inertia (53.2 to 2.5); going to 3 barely helps. Choose k = 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Silhouette of the point 3:</strong> a = its mean distance to its own cluster mates (1 and 2) = (2 + 1) / 2 = 1.5. b = its mean distance to the nearest other cluster (8 and 9) = (5 + 6) / 2 = 5.5. s = (b − a) / max(a, b) = 4 / 5.5 ≈ <strong>0.73</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Silhouette of the point 1:</strong> a = 1.5, b = 7.5, s = 0.80. It sits more firmly inside its cluster than 3 does.</li>\n<li><strong>Read the scale.</strong> Close to 1: clearly in the right cluster. Close to 0: on the border. Negative: probably in the wrong cluster.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Inertia always falls as k grows, so look for the elbow. Silhouette checks each point&#39;s fit and penalises clusters that are close together.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-algorithms-compared","text":"Algorithms compared","level":3},{"id":"h-choosing-k","text":"Choosing k","level":3},{"id":"h-practicalities","text":"Practicalities","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Idea</th><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>K-Means</strong></td><td>Assign to nearest centroid; update centroids; repeat</td><td>Fast, simple</td><td>Needs k, assumes spherical equal-size clusters, sensitive to scale/outliers/init (use k-means++)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hierarchical (agglomerative)</strong></td><td>Merge closest clusters; dendrogram</td><td>No k upfront, structure view</td><td>O(n^2) memory/time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DBSCAN</strong></td><td>Density-based: core points within <code>eps</code> and <code>min_samples</code></td><td>Finds arbitrary shapes, detects noise</td><td>Struggles with varying density; eps tuning</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GMM</strong></td><td>Soft clustering with Gaussians via EM</td><td>Soft assignments, elliptical clusters</td><td>Needs k, sensitive to init</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Choosing k:</strong> elbow, silhouette, gap statistic, business usability.</li>\n<li><strong>Always scale features</strong>; consider PCA before clustering in high dimensions.</li>\n<li><strong>Validate:</strong> silhouette, stability across runs, profiling clusters on business variables.</li>\n<li><strong>Use cases:</strong> customer segmentation (RFM), store clustering, content grouping.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-algorithms-compared\">Algorithms compared</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Idea</th><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>K-means</strong></td><td>assign to the nearest centroid, update centroids, repeat</td><td>fast, simple, scales well</td><td>needs k; assumes spherical, similar-sized clusters; sensitive to scale, outliers and initialisation (use <strong>k-means++</strong> and several restarts)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hierarchical (agglomerative)</strong></td><td>repeatedly merge the closest clusters; dendrogram</td><td>no k upfront; shows structure</td><td>O(n²) memory and time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DBSCAN</strong></td><td>clusters are dense regions: a core point has at least <code>min_samples</code> neighbours within <code>eps</code></td><td>arbitrary shapes, detects <strong>noise/outliers</strong></td><td>struggles with varying density; tuning <code>eps</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gaussian mixture (GMM)</strong></td><td>soft assignments with Gaussians fitted by EM</td><td>soft probabilities, elliptical clusters</td><td>needs k; sensitive to initialisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>DBSCAN vs k-means on two interleaving half-moons:</strong> k-means cuts the moons in half (adjusted Rand index 0.23), while DBSCAN with <code>eps = 0.2</code> recovers both moons perfectly (ARI 1.0), because the clusters are connected dense regions, not round blobs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-choosing-k\">Choosing k</h3>\n<p>On four well-separated blobs:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>k</th><th>Inertia (within-cluster sum of squares)</th><th>Silhouette</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2</td><td>4,627</td><td>0.64</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1,875</td><td>0.66</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td><strong>398</strong></td><td><strong>0.78</strong></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>356</td><td>0.67</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>318</td><td>0.55</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>283</td><td>0.44</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The <strong>elbow</strong> method looks for where inertia stops dropping sharply (here at 4); the <strong>silhouette</strong> peaks at 4 (0.78). The <strong>gap statistic</strong> compares with random data. Always add <strong>business usability</strong>: can the marketing team run four different campaigns? Explore:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"kmeans","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-practicalities\">Practicalities</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scale features</strong> (distance-based): standardise; consider <strong>PCA</strong> first in high dimensions.</li>\n<li>Handle categoricals with k-modes or a distance for mixed data (Gower) rather than forcing one-hot into k-means.</li>\n<li><strong>Validate</strong> with silhouette, stability across random seeds and subsamples, and <strong>profile</strong> clusters on business variables (average spend, recency, channel).</li>\n<li>Use cases: <strong>RFM customer segmentation</strong>, store clustering for assortment, grouping similar content or support tickets.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.cluster <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> KMeans\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.preprocessing <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> StandardScaler\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.metrics <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> silhouette_score\n\nZ = StandardScaler().fit_transform(rfm[[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;recency&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;frequency&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;monetary&quot;</span>]])\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> k <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">8</span>):\n    km = KMeans(k, n_init=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>).fit(Z)\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(k, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">round</span>(km.inertia_, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>), <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">round</span>(silhouette_score(Z, km.labels_), <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>))\nrfm[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;segment&quot;</span>] = KMeans(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, n_init=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>).fit_predict(Z)\nrfm.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;segment&quot;</span>).agg([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mean&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;</span>])                      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># profile each segment</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m9-clustering~1","q":"Your k-means segments change every time you rerun the notebook. Is the method broken?","a":"<p>K-means converges to a <strong>local optimum</strong> that depends on the random start. Use <code>init=&quot;k-means++&quot;</code>, a large <code>n_init</code> (for example 20) and a fixed <code>random_state</code>; check <strong>stability</strong> by comparing labels across runs (adjusted Rand index) or subsamples. If the segments are genuinely unstable, the data may lack natural cluster structure, so use fewer clusters, a different algorithm or features, or treat the result as a soft segmentation.</p>\n","s":"No: random initialisation; fix the seed, use k-means++ with multiple restarts, and check stability."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-clustering~ca0_1","q":"Customers with very different shapes of groups; K-means gives odd results. Alternative?","a":"<p>DBSCAN or a Gaussian mixture model, which handle non-spherical, differently sized clusters.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-clustering~ca0_2","q":"How do you pick k for K-means?","a":"<p>Elbow in the inertia curve, silhouette score, plus whether the segments are actionable and stable.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-clustering~ca0_3","q":"Why scale features before clustering?","a":"<p>Distances drive the grouping; a large-range feature such as income would dominate over age.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m9-clustering~ta1_1","q":"<p>Compute the silhouette of the point 8 in the same two-cluster solution ({1, 2, 3} and {8, 9}).</p>\n","a":"<p>a = distance to its own cluster mate 9 = 1. b = mean distance to 1, 2 and 3 = (7 + 6 + 5) / 3 = 6. s = (6 − 1) / 6 ≈ 0.83. Point 8 is clearly in the right cluster.</p>\n"}]},"m9-recommenders":{"id":"m9-recommenders","slug":"recommender-systems","m":9,"title":"Recommender systems","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":[],"tags":["recommender-systems","ranking"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Recommender system approaches (popularity, content-based, collaborative filtering, matrix factorisation, hybrid and two-stage retrieval-ranking), cold start, evaluation and pitfalls, with a worked item-item example.","keyTerms":["popularity baseline","content-based","collaborative filtering","matrix factorization","implicit feedback","cold start","two-tower","learning to rank","NDCG"],"interviewQs":["Explain collaborative filtering and its limitations.","How do you handle cold start?","How do you evaluate a recommender offline and online?","How does a two-stage recommender work at scale?"],"mistakes":["Random train-test splits that leak time","Ignoring popularity bias and feedback loops","Forgetting a popularity baseline"],"practice":"Build a popularity baseline, an item-item collaborative filter and a matrix factorisation model on a small ratings dataset and compare Precision@K.","related":["m8-other-metrics","m10-nlp","m14-portfolio"],"points":"Approach - How - Notes\nPopularity baseline - Recommend top-selling - Strong baseline; always include\nContent-based - Item features and user profile similarity (TF-IDF, embeddings, cosine) - No cold-start for items; limited novelty\nCollaborative filtering (user-user / item-item) - Similarity from interaction matrix - Sparse, cold start, scaling issues\nMatrix factorization (SVD, ALS) - Learn latent user/item vectors; score = dot product - Handles sparsity; implicit feedback via ALS\nHybrid / two-tower / deep - Combine features and embeddings - Industry standard at scale\nLearning-to-rank - Retrieval (candidate gen) then ranking (GBM/NN) - How Flipkart/Swiggy-scale systems work\n\n- Cold start: new user (popularity, onboarding questions, context/geo), new item (content features).\n- Implicit vs explicit feedback: clicks/views/purchases vs ratings.\n- Metrics: offline Precision@K, Recall@K, MAP, NDCG, coverage, diversity; online CTR, conversion, GMV, A/B test.\n- Pitfalls: popularity bias, feedback loops, leakage via random splits (use time-based splits)."},"intuitive":"<p>A recommender guesses what you will like. The easiest guess: &quot;most people like the popular things&quot; (a surprisingly tough baseline). Better: &quot;people who liked what you liked also liked this&quot; (collaborative filtering) or &quot;this item is similar to things you liked&quot; (content-based). Matrix factorisation learns a short secret profile for every user and item so that matching profiles predict a high rating. Large companies use two stages: a quick first pass picks a few hundred candidates, then a richer model ranks them.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Flipkart, Swiggy and Netflix-style problems appear in case rounds and projects.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Start with popularity, then collaborative filtering or factorisation, add features, evaluate with ranking metrics and A/B tests.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A recommender suggests items a user will like. Content-based methods recommend items similar to what the user liked, using item features. Collaborative filtering uses behaviour: users who liked what you liked also liked this item. Matrix factorisation learns a small vector per user and per item so that their dot product predicts the rating. Real systems use two stages: candidate generation to pick hundreds out of millions, then a ranking model. Cold start, new users or items with no history, is handled with popularity or content features. Metrics are precision at K, recall at K and NDCG offline, then A/B tests. The catch: popularity bias, and feedback loops that narrow what users see.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"A recommendation as a dot product","html":"<p>Matrix factorisation describes each user and each dish with a few learned numbers, here two: &quot;spicy&quot; and &quot;sweet&quot;.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>spicy</th><th>sweet</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>User Arjun</td><td>0.9</td><td>0.1</td></tr><tr><td>User Kavya</td><td>0.2</td><td>0.8</td></tr><tr><td>Biryani</td><td>1.0</td><td>0.1</td></tr><tr><td>Gulab jamun</td><td>0.1</td><td>1.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Predicted liking = the dot product of the user and dish vectors.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Arjun and biryani:</strong> 0.9 × 1.0 + 0.1 × 0.1 = <strong>0.91</strong>. Arjun and gulab jamun: 0.9 × 0.1 + 0.1 × 1.0 = <strong>0.19</strong>. Recommend biryani.</li>\n<li><strong>Kavya:</strong> biryani 0.2 × 1.0 + 0.8 × 0.1 = 0.28; gulab jamun 0.2 × 0.1 + 0.8 × 1.0 = <strong>0.82</strong>. Recommend dessert.</li>\n<li><strong>Where the numbers come from.</strong> Nobody types them in. The model learns them so that the dot products match the ratings or orders we have already seen; the two dimensions only resemble &quot;spicy&quot; and &quot;sweet&quot; here to make the idea readable.</li>\n<li><strong>Cold start.</strong> A brand-new user has no learned vector. Start with popular dishes, or with a vector built from their first few clicks or from onboarding answers, and switch to their learned vector as data arrives.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Collaborative filtering compresses everyone&#39;s history into short vectors, and the match between a user and an item is a dot product.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-approaches-compared","text":"Approaches compared","level":3},{"id":"h-a-tiny-worked-example-item-item-collaborative-fi","text":"A tiny worked example: item-item collaborative filtering","level":3},{"id":"h-feedback-and-cold-start","text":"Feedback and cold start","level":3},{"id":"h-two-stage-architecture","text":"Two-stage architecture","level":3},{"id":"h-evaluation","text":"Evaluation","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Popularity baseline</strong></td><td>Recommend top-selling</td><td>Strong baseline; always include</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Content-based</strong></td><td>Item features and user profile similarity (TF-IDF, embeddings, cosine)</td><td>No cold-start for items; limited novelty</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collaborative filtering (user-user / item-item)</strong></td><td>Similarity from interaction matrix</td><td>Sparse, cold start, scaling issues</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Matrix factorization (SVD, ALS)</strong></td><td>Learn latent user/item vectors; score = dot product</td><td>Handles sparsity; implicit feedback via ALS</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hybrid / two-tower / deep</strong></td><td>Combine features and embeddings</td><td>Industry standard at scale</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Learning-to-rank</strong></td><td>Retrieval (candidate gen) then ranking (GBM/NN)</td><td>How Flipkart/Swiggy-scale systems work</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Cold start:</strong> new user (popularity, onboarding questions, context/geo), new item (content features).</li>\n<li><strong>Implicit vs explicit feedback:</strong> clicks/views/purchases vs ratings.</li>\n<li><strong>Metrics:</strong> offline Precision@K, Recall@K, MAP, NDCG, coverage, diversity; online CTR, conversion, GMV, A/B test.</li>\n<li><strong>Pitfalls:</strong> popularity bias, feedback loops, leakage via random splits (use time-based splits).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-approaches-compared\">Approaches compared</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Popularity baseline</strong></td><td>recommend top sellers</td><td>strong baseline; always include it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Content-based</strong></td><td>similarity between item features and the user&#39;s profile (TF-IDF, embeddings, cosine)</td><td>no item cold start; limited novelty</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collaborative filtering</strong> (user-user, item-item)</td><td>similarity from the interaction matrix</td><td>no item features needed; sparse, cold start, scaling</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Matrix factorisation</strong> (SVD, ALS)</td><td>learn latent user and item vectors; score = dot product</td><td>handles sparsity; <strong>ALS</strong> suits implicit feedback</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hybrid / two-tower / deep</strong></td><td>combine features and embeddings</td><td>industry standard at scale</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Learning to rank</strong></td><td>retrieval (candidate generation) then ranking (GBM or neural net)</td><td>how large marketplaces work</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-tiny-worked-example-item-item-collaborative-fi\">A tiny worked example: item-item collaborative filtering</h3>\n<p>Five users rate five items (0 = not rated):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Item A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>D</th><th>E</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>U1</td><td>5</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>U2</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>U3</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>5</td><td>4</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>U4</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>U5</td><td>5</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Cosine similarity between item columns: A and B are similar (0.75), while C, D and E form a group (similarities 0.94 to 0.98). U5 has rated A (5) and D (1) but not B. Predict B as a similarity-weighted average of U5&#39;s ratings: <code>(0.75 × 5 + 0.19 × 1) / (0.75 + 0.19) ≈ 4.2</code>, so B is recommended (it resembles A, which U5 loved).</p>\n<p><strong>Matrix factorisation</strong> finds low-rank approximations: with 2 latent factors the SVD reconstruction of U5&#39;s row is about <code>[3.3, 2.4, 0.3, 0.5, 0.1]</code>, giving the unrated item B a clearly higher score (2.4) than the C/D/E group (0.3, 0.5, 0.1), consistent with the user&#39;s taste.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\nU, S, Vt = np.linalg.svd(R, full_matrices=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)\nR2 = U[:, :<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>] * S[:<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>] @ Vt[:<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>]                     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># rank-2 reconstruction: predicted affinity for every user-item pair</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>(Real systems factor a sparse matrix with missing entries treated as unknown, not zero, using ALS or SGD, often with implicit-feedback confidence weights.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-feedback-and-cold-start\">Feedback and cold start</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Explicit</strong> (ratings) is scarce; <strong>implicit</strong> (clicks, views, purchases, dwell time) is abundant but noisy (a click is not a like; absence is not dislike).</li>\n<li><strong>Cold start:</strong> <em>new user</em>: popularity, onboarding questions, context (location, time, device); <em>new item</em>: content features, embeddings, exploration (bandits).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-two-stage-architecture\">Two-stage architecture</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Candidate generation / retrieval:</strong> from millions to hundreds quickly using two-tower embeddings and approximate nearest-neighbour search (FAISS), collaborative filtering, popularity and rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Ranking:</strong> a richer model (gradient boosting or a neural net) scores candidates using user, item and context features (time, location, weather) to predict click or order probability.</li>\n<li><strong>Re-ranking:</strong> business rules, diversity, freshness, fairness, deduplication.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-evaluation\">Evaluation</h3>\n<p><strong>Offline:</strong> Precision@K, Recall@K, MAP, NDCG, coverage, diversity. Split <strong>by time</strong> (train on the past, test on later interactions) because a random split leaks future behaviour. <strong>Online:</strong> CTR, conversion, GMV, retention through an <strong>A/B test</strong>; offline gains often do not transfer.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfalls:</strong> <strong>popularity bias</strong> (the rich get richer), <strong>feedback loops</strong> (recommendations shape the data they are trained on), filter bubbles, and position bias in click logs.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m9-recommenders~1","q":"How would you build &quot;restaurants you might like&quot; for a food-delivery app?","a":"<p><strong>Retrieval</strong> (hundreds of candidates): restaurants within delivery range, popular and newly opened ones, collaborative filtering/embeddings from past orders, same-cuisine lookalikes. <strong>Ranking:</strong> a gradient-boosted or neural model predicting order probability from user history (cuisine affinity, price band, time of day), restaurant features (rating, prep time, discounts), and context (time, weather, distance, ETA). <strong>Business layers:</strong> diversity across cuisines, exploration for new restaurants, rules for closed or overloaded restaurants. <strong>Evaluation:</strong> offline NDCG@10 with a time split, then an A/B test on conversion, GMV, repeat rate and delivery-time guardrails.</p>\n","s":"Two-stage: retrieval with popularity, collaborative and geo filters; a ranker on user, restaurant and context features; evaluate with NDCG and an A/B test."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-recommenders~ca0_1","q":"A new user has no history. What do you recommend?","a":"<p>Popular or trending items, items by context (location, device), then ask for preferences; switch to personalised as signals arrive.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-recommenders~ca0_2","q":"Content-based versus collaborative filtering?","a":"<p>Content-based uses item attributes (no other users needed, narrower); collaborative uses behaviour patterns (discovers surprising items, needs data and has cold start).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-recommenders~ca0_3","q":"Why two stages in large recommenders?","a":"<p>Scoring millions of items with a heavy model is too slow; a cheap retrieval step narrows to hundreds for the precise ranker.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m9-recommenders~ta1_1","q":"<p>For one user, the model recommends 5 items in order; the user later bought items 2 and 5 from that list, plus one item that was not recommended. Compute precision@5 and recall@5.</p>\n","a":"<p>Precision@5 = relevant items in the top 5 / 5 = 2/5 = 0.4. Recall@5 = relevant items found / all relevant items = 2/3 ≈ 0.67. A ranking metric such as NDCG would also reward putting the bought items higher than positions 2 and 5.</p>\n"}]},"m9-anomaly":{"id":"m9-anomaly","slug":"anomaly-detection","m":9,"title":"Anomaly detection","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":[],"tags":["anomaly-detection","unsupervised"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Anomaly detection methods (statistical, Isolation Forest, LOF, autoencoders), how to evaluate them with scarce labels, and how to combine them with rules.","keyTerms":["z-score","IQR","Isolation Forest","One-Class SVM","Local Outlier Factor","autoencoder","precision at K","delayed labels"],"interviewQs":["How does Isolation Forest detect anomalies?","How do you evaluate an anomaly detector when labels are scarce?","When would you prefer a rule-based approach?"],"mistakes":["Using accuracy","Ignoring that labels are delayed (chargebacks)","Flagging too many alerts for the review team"],"practice":"Inject 10 outliers into 500 normal points, and compare z-score, IQR and Isolation Forest.","related":["m5-bayes","m8-imbalanced","m12-key-concepts"],"points":"- Statistical (z-score, IQR, Grubbs), Isolation Forest (anomalies isolated with fewer splits), One-Class SVM, Local Outlier Factor, autoencoders (high reconstruction error), time-series residual methods, rule-based + ML hybrids.\n- Evaluate with precision@K, recall at fixed review capacity, PR-AUC; labels are scarce and delayed (chargebacks)."},"intuitive":"<p>An anomaly is something that does not look like the rest. The simplest detectors ask &quot;is this number far from the average?&quot; (z-score). Isolation Forest uses a clever trick: to isolate a weird point you need very few random cuts (it is alone in a far corner), while a normal point in the crowd needs many cuts. In practice you also have to decide how many alerts a human team can actually review, so anomaly detection is as much about capacity as about maths.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Fraud, system monitoring and data quality all need it, usually with very few labels.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Start with simple statistics and rules, add Isolation Forest, and measure by precision within a review budget.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Anomaly detection finds rare, unusual points like fraud or sensor faults. Simple statistical rules come first: a z-score flags values more than three standard deviations from the mean, and the IQR rule flags values beyond one and a half interquartile ranges from the quartiles, which is robust to outliers. Isolation Forest isolates points with random splits; anomalies need fewer splits. Autoencoders learn to reconstruct normal data, and high reconstruction error signals oddness. With few labels, I evaluate by precision at K and by review of flagged cases. The catch: define what &#39;anomalous&#39; means for the business, and expect drift to change what is normal.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Flag an odd day against its own weekday","html":"<p>Orders on the last four Mondays: 100, 104, 98, 102. Today, a Monday, there are 80. Is that unusual?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Compare like with like.</strong> Mondays have their own level, so compare today with recent Mondays, not with the whole week.</li>\n<li><strong>Robust centre:</strong> the median of 100, 104, 98, 102 is <strong>101</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Robust spread:</strong> the median absolute deviation. Distances from 101 are 1, 3, 3, 1, with median <strong>2</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Robust z-score:</strong> 0.6745 × (80 − 101) / 2 ≈ <strong>−7.1</strong>. A common rule flags values beyond ±3.5, so today is a strong anomaly.</li>\n<li><strong>Before alerting, check the cheap explanations:</strong> a holiday, an app outage, a tracking change, a city-level event. Many &quot;anomalies&quot; are data problems.</li>\n<li><strong>Tune the alert.</strong> If this rule fires five times a week and four are false, people stop reading it. Measure precision of the alerts over a month and adjust the threshold.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Anomaly detection is &quot;compare with the right normal, using robust statistics, and keep false alarms rare&quot;.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-methods","text":"Methods","level":3},{"id":"h-comparison-on-synthetic-data","text":"Comparison on synthetic data","level":3},{"id":"h-evaluating-with-scarce-and-delayed-labels","text":"Evaluating with scarce and delayed labels","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Statistical (z-score, IQR, Grubbs), <strong>Isolation Forest</strong> (anomalies isolated with fewer splits), One-Class SVM, Local Outlier Factor, autoencoders (high reconstruction error), time-series residual methods, rule-based + ML hybrids.</li>\n<li>Evaluate with precision@K, recall at fixed review capacity, PR-AUC; labels are scarce and delayed (chargebacks).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-methods\">Methods</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Idea</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>z-score, IQR</strong></td><td>flag values far from the centre</td><td>one variable, roughly stable distribution</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Isolation Forest</strong></td><td>random trees isolate anomalies in <strong>fewer splits</strong> (short average path length = more anomalous)</td><td>tabular, many features, fast, no scaling needed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>One-Class SVM</strong></td><td>learn a boundary around normal data</td><td>small data; sensitive to scaling and parameters</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Local Outlier Factor (LOF)</strong></td><td>compare a point&#39;s local density with its neighbours&#39;</td><td>clusters of varying density</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Autoencoders</strong></td><td>reconstruct normal data; high reconstruction error means anomaly</td><td>high-dimensional (images, sensor data)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Time series residuals</strong></td><td>forecast, then flag large residuals</td><td>seasonal metrics</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rules + ML hybrid</strong></td><td>known patterns as rules, ML for the unknown</td><td>production fraud systems</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-comparison-on-synthetic-data\">Comparison on synthetic data</h3>\n<p>500 normal 2-D points plus 10 uniform outliers spread over a wide box: Isolation Forest (contamination 2%) flags 11 points and catches 7 of the 10 injected outliers (those that fall inside the normal cloud are genuinely indistinguishable). A per-feature <strong>z-score &gt; 3</strong> flags only 4, because it looks at one axis at a time and misses points that are unusual only in combination.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.ensemble <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> IsolationForest\niso = IsolationForest(contamination=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.02</span>, random_state=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>).fit(X)\nlabels = iso.predict(X)                  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># -1 = anomaly</span>\nscores = -iso.score_samples(X)           <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># higher = more anomalous (use this to rank)</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-evaluating-with-scarce-and-delayed-labels\">Evaluating with scarce and delayed labels</h3>\n<p>Labels are rare and arrive late (a chargeback may come 30 days after the transaction). Evaluate with <strong>precision@K</strong> (of the K highest-scoring alerts, how many were real), <strong>recall at a fixed review capacity</strong>, PR-AUC on the labelled portion, and <strong>lift over random</strong>. Use analyst feedback to create a feedback loop: confirmed cases become labels for a supervised model later. Monitor alert volume and drift.</p>\n<p><strong>Practical design:</strong> combine simple rules (velocity limits, blacklists), an unsupervised score for novelty, and a supervised model as labels accumulate; calibrate the threshold to the review team&#39;s capacity.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m9-anomaly~1","q":"An Isolation Forest flags 5% of transactions but the fraud team can review only 0.5%. What do you do?","a":"<p>The threshold should come from <strong>capacity and cost</strong>, not a fixed contamination rate: sort by anomaly score and send only the top 0.5% to reviewers (measure precision@K on confirmed cases), automate low-risk handling (step-up authentication for the next tier), and improve the model with reviewer feedback and additional features (velocity, device, geography). Track precision, loss prevented and customer friction.</p>\n","s":"Rank by anomaly score and review only the top 0.5%; tune and evaluate precision within that budget."},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout example\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Worked example</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p><strong>Z-score and IQR by hand.</strong> Daily orders: 98, 102, 100, 101, 99, 103, 97, 100, 180. Mean ≈ 108.9, SD ≈ 26.7 (the outlier inflates both). z for 180 = (180 - 108.9)/26.7 ≈ 2.7, below 3, so the z rule misses it. IQR rule: Q1 = 98.5, Q3 = 102.5 (approx.), IQR = 4, upper fence = 102.5 + 1.5×4 = 108.5, so 180 is flagged clearly. Lesson: an outlier distorts the mean and SD it is judged against, so robust rules (median, IQR) catch it better.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-anomaly~ca0_1","q":"Why can the z-score miss an obvious outlier?","a":"<p>The outlier itself inflates the mean and standard deviation, shrinking its own z-score; use robust statistics like median and MAD or IQR.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-anomaly~ca0_2","q":"Why does Isolation Forest isolate anomalies quickly?","a":"<p>Anomalies are few and different, so random splits separate them in fewer steps (shorter path length).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m9-anomaly~ca0_3","q":"Labels are almost nonexistent. How do you evaluate an anomaly detector?","a":"<p>Have experts review the top-K flagged cases (precision at K), inject synthetic anomalies, and track findings and false alarms over time.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m9-anomaly~ta1_1","q":"<p>A fraud team can review 20 cases a day. An isolation forest flags 200 transactions per day. What should you do, and what metric tells you whether the model is good?</p>\n","a":"<p>Rank by anomaly score and send only the top 20 a day for review (capacity sets the cut-off), with the threshold set accordingly. Measure precision@20: the share of reviewed cases that turn out to be real fraud. Track it over weeks, and use the confirmed cases to improve the model, for example by moving to a supervised model once enough labels exist.</p>\n"}]}});